{{see also|North Carolina's 1st congressional district}}
The new 1st district includes all of [[Bertie County, North Carolina|Bertie]], [[Camden County, North Carolina|Camden]], [[Chowan County, North Carolina|Chowan]], [[Currituck County, North Carolina|Currituck]], [[Edgecombe County, North Carolina|Edgecombe]], [[Gates County, North Carolina|Gates]], [[Greene County, North Carolina|Greene]], [[Halifax County, North Carolina|Halifax]], [[Hertford County, North Carolina|Hertford]], [[Lenoir, North Carolina|Lenoir]], [[Martin County, North Carolina|Martin]], [[Nash County, North Carolina|Nash]], [[Northampton County, North Carolina|Northampton]], [[Pasquotank County, North Carolina|Pasquotank]], [[Perquimans County, North Carolina|Perquimans]], [[Tyrrell County, North Carolina|Tyrrell]], [[Vance County, North Carolina|Vance]], [[Warren County, North Carolina|Warren]], [[Wayne County, North Carolina|Wayne]], [[Washington County, North Carolina|Washington]], and [[Wilson County, North Carolina|Wilson]] counties, as well as a small portion of eastern [[Granville County, North Carolina|Granville County]]. The incumbent was Democrat [[Don Davis (North Carolina politician)|Don Davis]], who was previously elected with 52.4% of the vote in 2022.<ref name="HPVT">{{cite web |title=2022 National House Vote Tracker |url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/charts/house-charts/national-house-vote-tracker/2022 |access-date=December 30, 2023 |website=[[The Cook Political Report with Amy Walter|Cook Political Report]]}}</ref>

