{{see also|North Carolina's 6th congressional district}}
The new 6th district includes all of [[Davidson County, North Carolina|Davidson]], [[Davie County, North Carolina|Davie]], and [[Rowan County, North Carolina|Rowan]] counties, as well as portions of northwestern [[Cabarrus County, North Carolina|Cabarrus County]], western [[Forsyth County, North Carolina|Forsyth County]], and southwestern [[Guilford County, North Carolina|Guilford County]]. Prior to redistricting, the incumbent was Democrat [[Kathy Manning]]; however, Manning was drawn out of the 6th district and into the 5th, leaving the district with no incumbent, as Manning instead chose to retire. [[Addison McDowell]] was the Republican nominee for this district, after he received the most votes in the March primary and former congressman [[Mark Walker (North Carolina politician)|Mark Walker]] withdrew from the race, forgoing a runoff election.<ref name = uncontested>{{cite news |work=[[The Associated Press]] |date=March 13, 2024 |access-date=March 13, 2024 |url=https://apnews.com/article/north-carolina-primary-election-house-runoff-mcdowell-manning-walker-590396ae388be2071e49fe159e798821 |author=Robertson, Gary |title=McDowell now uncontested in North Carolina House race after ex-Rep. Walker declines runoff}}</ref>

As of 2025, Constitution Party candidate for this district Kevin Hayes is the best performing US House candidate in the party's history, in terms of both percentage and raw vote total.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-11 |title=Kevin Hayes, Constitution Party U.S. House Nominee in North Carolina, Sets Many Records {{!}} |url=https://ballot-access.org/2024/11/11/kevin-hayes-constitution-party-u-s-house-nominee-in-north-carolina-sets-many-records/ |access-date=2024-11-27 |language=en-US}}</ref>

